beszel agent @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (@ARCH@) - vms-beszel
========================================================

Reports this OpenVMS system to a beszel hub (https://beszel.dev), version
0.21.0: CPU, memory, swap, disks, network and load, with the hub's charts,
history and alerts.  The agent connects out to the hub; nothing listens on
this system.  It runs as its own unprivileged account (NETMBX and TMPMBX only)
and refuses to run as SYSTEM or with privileges.

Requires: OpenVMS x86-64 (tested on E9.2-4) or Integrity (tested on
V8.4-2L3), VSI TCP/IP Services, and VSI's SSL3 kit.

Setting up (as SYSTEM)
----------------------
1. In the hub, click "Add System".  Note the public key (ssh-ed25519 ...) and
   the token it shows; ignore its install commands, which are for Linux.

2. Create the agent's account and set the protections, once:

     $ @BESZEL$ROOT:[COM]BESZEL$CONFIGURE

   It asks for the account (default BESZEL) and, for a new account, its UIC:
   choose a group of its own, above MAXSYSGROUP (e.g. [377,1]).  The account
   gets NETMBX and TMPMBX only and can't log in by any route; the agent still
   runs as it.  Run it again at any time: it changes only an account it
   created.

3. Edit BESZEL$ROOT:[CONF]AGENT.CONF (made from AGENT.CONF_TEMPLATE, which
   explains every setting).  Three are required:

     HUB_URL=https://beszel.example.com
     KEY="ssh-ed25519 AAAA..."
     TOKEN=...

   The file holds the token: keep it readable by the account only.  EDIT
   makes a new version each time, so afterwards:

     $ SET FILE/OWNER=BESZEL/PROTECTION=(S:RWED,O:RWED,G,W) -
           BESZEL$ROOT:[CONF]AGENT.CONF;*
     $ PURGE BESZEL$ROOT:[CONF]AGENT.CONF

   Check what it would report, without contacting the hub:

     $ AGENT := $BESZEL$ROOT:[BIN]BESZEL_AGENT.EXE
     $ AGENT --SAMPLE 10

4. Start it:

     $ @BESZEL$ROOT:[COM]BESZEL$CONTROL START

   Within a minute the system shows as up in the hub.  STOP, RESTART (after
   changing AGENT.CONF) and STATUS work the same way.

5. At boot and shutdown, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM (after TCP/IP)
   and SYS$MANAGER:SYSHUTDWN.COM:

     $ @SYS$STARTUP:BESZEL$STARTUP.COM
     $ @SYS$STARTUP:BESZEL$SHUTDOWN.COM

Files
-----
  BESZEL$ROOT:[BIN]   BESZEL_AGENT.EXE, and BESZEL_LAUNCH.EXE (starts the agent
                      as its account)
  BESZEL$ROOT:[COM]   BESZEL$CONTROL.COM, BESZEL$RUN.COM (restarts the agent if
                      it stops), BESZEL$CONFIGURE.COM
  BESZEL$ROOT:[CONF]  AGENT.CONF, AGENT.CONF_TEMPLATE, CACERT.PEM (the public
                      CAs, for https hubs)
  BESZEL$ROOT:[DATA]  the fingerprint, which ties the agent to its system in
                      the hub: don't delete it
  BESZEL$ROOT:[LOG]   BESZEL_AGENT.LOG (five versions kept)
  SYS$MANAGER:BESZEL$CONFIG.COM   the account, and whether to start at boot

PRODUCT REMOVE BESZEL stops the agent and deassigns BESZEL$ROOT and
BESZEL$USER.  AGENT.CONF, the fingerprint, the logs, the site settings and the
account are kept, so a reinstall carries on where it left off.

When it doesn't connect, BESZEL$ROOT:[LOG]BESZEL_AGENT.LOG says why.  The
messages are explained at https://github.com/issinoho/vms-beszel.

